{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nBoth review gates execute criterion work through runners whose return values cannot reliably distinguish clean execution from failed, cancelled, skipped, budget-shed, or resumed work. Plan-review failures can become empty successful checkpoints, while code review loses earlier successful work when a later criterion raises. A shared effective-policy snapshot alone cannot fix those execution facts.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a narrow shared discovery kernel under `rebar.llm.review_kernel`. Define immutable `DiscoveryStagePlan` and `DiscoveryUnitPlan` inputs plus `DiscoveryStageResult` and typed per-unit outcomes: `success`, `resumed`, `skipped`, `shed`, `failed`, and `cancelled`. A plan carries stable unit ids, dependencies, prompt/contract/model/mode identities, context and policy digests, blocking capability, budget estimate, and gate-supplied execution callbacks.\n\nThe executor owns dependency-aware scheduling, explicit positive-budget shedding, usage accumulation, cancellation observation, and success-only checkpoint envelope creation. After RP-01 exhausts a local operation it preserves successful independent results and continues; a dependent of missing work becomes `skipped` with a dependency reason. Systemic configuration/provider initialization failures and cancellation abort remaining execution with exact completed-so-far accounting. Gate verdicts remain outside the executor.\n\nBudget shedding is deterministic and reproducible: after dependency resolution, units whose estimate does not fit the positive budget are shed in a fixed order — ascending by dependency topological rank, then by stable unit id — so two executors given the same plan and the same budget shed exactly the same units. A shed of a unit also sheds its unmet dependents (recorded `skipped`), never a success.\n\nCancellation has a well-defined cut point against the existing non-interruptible runner (RP-01 documents that in-flight provider calls are sunk cost and complete after a cancel signal). The cut is dispatch: a unit whose model call was already dispatched when cancellation is observed runs to completion and is committed as its true outcome (`success` on digest-complete content, `failed` on error), with its real usage counted — completed work is never dropped or relabelled, so accounting stays exact and usage is never fabricated. A unit not yet dispatched when cancellation is observed is recorded `cancelled` with a cancellation reason and never started. This makes AC3's \"exact completed/failed/cancelled accounting\" total and unambiguous.\n\nDefine a new versioned, digest-complete checkpoint/trace envelope. Its identity covers material, code/ref, effective-policy snapshot, prompt and contract, model/mode, topology, input context, dependencies, and namespace version. Only `success` and content-identical `resumed` units may be committed as reusable successes; failed, cancelled, skipped, and shed units remain missing. Legacy checkpoints are never read as new successes.\n\nThe typed trace is safe stored diagnostic data: normalized ids, outcomes, reason codes, usage, and lineage only. It excludes raw prompts, ticket/context bodies, provider responses, and secrets. No per-unit trace is added to review or review-status response contracts.\n\n## Boundaries and Rationale\nKeep policy selection, gate context construction, YAML/BPMN topology, provider-turn retry/output handling, checkpoint persistence, and final verdict decisions in their current owners. A focused execution kernel gives both gates the same trustworthy facts without building a second workflow interpreter or fighting RP-01 provider semantics.\n\nThe per-unit dependency graph and `skipped`-with-dependency-reason outcome are not speculative: plan-review already composes dependent stages, where a synthesis/aggregation unit consumes earlier per-facet chunk results, and a synthesis unit that depends on a chunk that failed or was shed must become `skipped` (not silently emptied into a passing checkpoint) — the exact bug AC2 prevents. The kernel is the substrate onto which RP-06's later stories migrate both gates, so the dependency edge is exercised by that current plan-review pass→synthesis relationship rather than introduced ahead of any consumer. Where a stage has no inter-unit dependency, the graph is empty and scheduling degenerates to independent fan-out.\n\n## Testing\nUse in-process contract tests with the real plan/result/envelope codecs and an injected stateful fake only at the model-call boundary. Tests assert exact call order, returned outcomes, usage, checkpoint eligibility, collateral state, and a clean retry after injected local failure. Negative controls prove a systemic failure aborts and a failed/shed/cancelled result never round-trips as success. A cancellation test injects an in-flight call that completes after the cancel signal and asserts it is committed as its true outcome while a not-yet-dispatched sibling is recorded `cancelled`. A budget test asserts the deterministic shed set is identical across repeated runs of the same plan and budget.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `DiscoveryStagePlan` and `DiscoveryStageResult` represent stable unit identity, dependencies, blocking capability, usage, and all six approved outcome kinds without gate-specific verdict fields.\n- [ ] Given one exhausted local failure, the real executor retains earlier successes, executes later independent units, and marks dependent units skipped with the missing dependency id.\n- [ ] Given a systemic initialization/configuration failure or cancellation, the executor stops scheduling new work and returns exact completed, failed, and cancelled accounting without fabricating usage or findings; a unit whose non-interruptible model call was already dispatched when cancellation is observed is committed as its true `success`/`failed` outcome with real usage, while a not-yet-dispatched unit is recorded `cancelled`.\n- [ ] A positive explicit budget deterministically sheds work that does not fit — in ascending (dependency rank, unit id) order so the shed set is identical across runs of the same plan and budget; omission is uncapped and zero is rejected by the contract rather than interpreted as disablement.\n- [ ] The versioned envelope round-trips only digest-complete, content-identical success/resume records; injected failed, cancelled, skipped, shed, corrupt, and legacy envelopes remain missing.\n- [ ] Stored trace serialization contains normalized outcome metadata and lineage but no raw prompt, ticket/context body, provider payload, or secret, and public review result schemas remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Fault-injection tests prove restoration plus a clean rerun succeeds and that unrelated successful unit state is unchanged."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787346653878414001,"uuid":"7caf60be-a113-49d1-8a52-932933882cf0"}